# Everything under public/data is published byte-for-byte to GitHub Pages, and git
# stores it with LF. Keep the working copy LF too, so verify-live.mjs can compare the
# bytes it fetches from production against the file on disk.
#
# Without this, core.autocrlf=true rewrites these files to CRLF on checkout while
# production serves LF, so the delivery check reports "content differs" on a file whose
# parsed content is identical. picture-analysis.json hit exactly that: 1,433,125 bytes on
# disk vs 1,400,468 served — 32,657 CR bytes, zero real differences.
public/data/*.json text eol=lf

# THE SAME RULE, FOR EVERY ARTIFACT WHOSE BYTES ARE PINNED.
#
# The rule above covers public/data because production serves those bytes. It is not the only
# place bytes are compared: reviewed action sets under audit/ are pinned by raw sha256, so that a
# ruling cannot be edited between review and apply. apply-entity-cleanup.mjs pins Owner Ruling 3
# and the lane-B review; lib/step3b1Sets.mjs pins eight action sets; apply-step3b1.mjs pins its
# plan and held dispositions.
#
# Those pins hash the FILE, and core.autocrlf=true rewrites JSON to CRLF on checkout, so a brand
# new worktree of this repository could not run its own chain:
#
#   X OWNER RULING 3 IS NOT THE REVIEWED SET — stopped.
#      expected 65f82ace6748eaaf1bbbdd010f8cba30802c8f9a7c918e5928be0ef2a20e21ef
#      found    a09291ed06713bf7ad247331a3912c7f91f78e42f9a5ad5f537813f0ce3ad397
#
# Nothing was wrong with the ruling; 732 CR bytes had been added to it on the way out of git. The
# only way to work was to delete every tracked file and re-materialise it with core.autocrlf off,
# which is not a thing anyone should have to know.
#
# The blobs are already LF, so this changes no stored content — it only stops checkout from
# corrupting them on the way to disk.
audit/**/*.json   text eol=lf
audit/**/*.jsonl  text eol=lf

# The QA reports beside them are regenerated by the chain, so a CRLF checkout makes every run
# rewrite them and a brand new worktree is dirty the moment it builds. Their blobs are already LF
# — of 1,165 text-ish tracked files only four contain a CR, and none of those is under audit/ —
# so this normalises nothing that is stored.
audit/**/*.md     text eol=lf
audit/**/*.csv    text eol=lf
audit/**/*.tsv    text eol=lf
audit/**/*.txt    text eol=lf

# The identity registry and its evidence package are hashed the same way, by MANIFEST.json.
identity/*.json                          text eol=lf
QUESTION-IDENTITY-STABILIZATION/*.json   text eol=lf
QUESTION-IDENTITY-STABILIZATION/*.jsonl  text eol=lf
